      hallazgo: 'On 12 March 2025 El Observador reported that the Court of Auditors objected to the cooperation agreement between OSE and the National Development Corporation (CND) to manage the project, holding that it amounted to an "evasion of contracting procedures" and that the object of the contracts was not clear. The payment to CND was around $350 million — more than US$8 million — over five years, plus 4% and VAT monthly on funds executed, fees of $120,000 to $140,000 plus VAT per person recruited and $6,200 plus VAT per hour of technical advice. Court member Francisco Gallinal voted against the objection: he held that CND had the legal competence to execute the agreement and that its involvement reduced costs for OSE. In May 2026, before the parliamentary committee, OSE president Pablo Ferreri announced a reformulation: he said there was a US$260 million "funding mismatch", that the plan implied "an investment of more than 500 million dollars, when the loan obtained from Fonplata was 320 million", and that the towns were chosen "on no technical criterion", with surveys done by drone and without checks on the ground, to the point of including towns that already had networks.',
